            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-111767",
              "name": "CVE-2026-111767",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-111767: MCP TypeScript SDK: Experimental tasks were not tied to the session that created them"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Summary\nIn affected versions, the experimental tasks feature (`taskStore`) did not check which session created a task. Clients sharing one `InMemoryTaskStore` could list, read and cancel each other's tasks.\n\nExperimental tasks are deprecated. The 2026-07-28 specification replaced them with a redesigned extension. The best course of action is to stop using them.\n\n### Am I affected?\nOnly if your HTTP server passes `taskStore` to `McpServer` or `Server`. If `grep -ri taskstore --exclude-dir=node_modules .` finds nothing, you are not affected.\n\nAffected versions: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.24.0 through 1.31.0. The 2.x packages are not affected.\n\n### Fix\nUpgrade to `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later. Each task is now tied to the session that created it, so a client that starts a new session can no longer reach its earlier tasks.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade yet, remove `taskStore`, or create one `InMemoryTaskStore` per session.",
                "markdown": "### Summary\nIn affected versions, the experimental tasks feature (`taskStore`) did not check which session created a task. Clients sharing one `InMemoryTaskStore` could list, read and cancel each other's tasks.\n\nExperimental tasks are deprecated. The 2026-07-28 specification replaced them with a redesigned extension. The best course of action is to stop using them.\n\n### Am I affected?\nOnly if your HTTP server passes `taskStore` to `McpServer` or `Server`. If `grep -ri taskstore --exclude-dir=node_modules .` finds nothing, you are not affected.\n\nAffected versions: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.24.0 through 1.31.0. The 2.x packages are not affected.\n\n### Fix\nUpgrade to `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later. Each task is now tied to the session that created it, so a client that starts a new session can no longer reach its earlier tasks.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade yet, remove `taskStore`, or create one `InMemoryTaskStore` per session."
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111767",
                "GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111767](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111767)**.\n\n## [GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e In affected versions, the experimental tasks feature (`taskStore`) did not check which session created a task. Clients sharing one `InMemoryTaskStore` could list, read and cancel each other's tasks.\n\u003e \n\u003e Experimental tasks are deprecated. The 2026-07-28 specification replaced them with a redesigned extension. The best course of action is to stop using them.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Am I affected?\n\u003e Only if your HTTP server passes `taskStore` to `McpServer` or `Server`. If `grep -ri taskstore --exclude-dir=node_modules .` finds nothing, you are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected versions: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.24.0 through 1.31.0. The 2.x packages are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix\n\u003e Upgrade to `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later. Each task is now tied to the session that created it, so a client that starts a new session can no longer reach its earlier tasks.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade yet, remove `taskStore`, or create one `InMemoryTaskStore` per session.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.29.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.32.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111767\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111767](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111767)**.\n\n## [GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e In affected versions, the experimental tasks feature (`taskStore`) did not check which session created a task. Clients sharing one `InMemoryTaskStore` could list, read and cancel each other's tasks.\n\u003e \n\u003e Experimental tasks are deprecated. The 2026-07-28 specification replaced them with a redesigned extension. The best course of action is to stop using them.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Am I affected?\n\u003e Only if your HTTP server passes `taskStore` to `McpServer` or `Server`. If `grep -ri taskstore --exclude-dir=node_modules .` finds nothing, you are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected versions: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.24.0 through 1.31.0. The 2.x packages are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix\n\u003e Upgrade to `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later. Each task is now tied to the session that created it, so a client that starts a new session can no longer reach its earlier tasks.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade yet, remove `taskStore`, or create one `InMemoryTaskStore` per session.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.29.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.32.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111767\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-111181",
              "name": "CVE-2026-111181",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-111181: body-parser vulnerable to Denial of Service via trailing bytes after a compressed request body"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.",
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created."
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111181",
                "GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111181](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111181)**.\n\n## [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e A single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Pass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | body-parser | 2.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 | body-parser | 1.20.9, 2.4.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111181](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111181)**.\n\n## [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e A single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Pass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | body-parser | 2.3.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 | body-parser | 1.20.9, 2.4.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-110725",
              "name": "CVE-2026-110725",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-110725: fast-uri vulnerable to scheme injection via an unescaped first-segment colon in suffix references"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Impact\n\n`fast-uri`'s `serialize()`, `normalize()`, and `resolve()`, when called with the non-default option `reference: 'suffix'` on a reference that has no authority, emit a relative reference whose first path segment contains a bare colon without escaping it. RFC 3986 section 3.3 requires the first segment of a `path-noscheme` reference not to contain a colon, precisely because a bare colon makes the string reparse as an absolute URI with a scheme. `fast-uri`'s default serialization escapes that colon, but the suffix path does not.\n\nFor example, `normalize('javascript:alert(1)', { reference: 'suffix' })` returns `javascript:alert(1)`, which reparses as a live `javascript:` scheme; `data:` and `vbscript:` behave the same.\n\nApplications that serialize or normalize a relative reference with `reference: 'suffix'` and then treat the result as a URI can turn inert input into a live `javascript:`, `data:`, or `vbscript:` URL (a cross-site scripting vector), and `equal()` can conflate a scheme-bearing reference with a relative one. Exposure is limited to applications that use the non-default `reference: 'suffix'` option.\n\n### Patches\n\nUpgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDo not use the `reference: 'suffix'` option on untrusted input. If suffix output is required, percent-encode a colon in the first path segment of the result before treating it as a URI.",
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\n`fast-uri`'s `serialize()`, `normalize()`, and `resolve()`, when called with the non-default option `reference: 'suffix'` on a reference that has no authority, emit a relative reference whose first path segment contains a bare colon without escaping it. RFC 3986 section 3.3 requires the first segment of a `path-noscheme` reference not to contain a colon, precisely because a bare colon makes the string reparse as an absolute URI with a scheme. `fast-uri`'s default serialization escapes that colon, but the suffix path does not.\n\nFor example, `normalize('javascript:alert(1)', { reference: 'suffix' })` returns `javascript:alert(1)`, which reparses as a live `javascript:` scheme; `data:` and `vbscript:` behave the same.\n\nApplications that serialize or normalize a relative reference with `reference: 'suffix'` and then treat the result as a URI can turn inert input into a live `javascript:`, `data:`, or `vbscript:` URL (a cross-site scripting vector), and `equal()` can conflate a scheme-bearing reference with a relative one. Exposure is limited to applications that use the non-default `reference: 'suffix'` option.\n\n### Patches\n\nUpgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDo not use the `reference: 'suffix'` option on untrusted input. If suffix output is required, percent-encode a colon in the first path segment of the result before treating it as a URI."
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110725",
                "GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110725](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-110725)**.\n\n## [GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `fast-uri`'s `serialize()`, `normalize()`, and `resolve()`, when called with the non-default option `reference: 'suffix'` on a reference that has no authority, emit a relative reference whose first path segment contains a bare colon without escaping it. RFC 3986 section 3.3 requires the first segment of a `path-noscheme` reference not to contain a colon, precisely because a bare colon makes the string reparse as an absolute URI with a scheme. `fast-uri`'s default serialization escapes that colon, but the suffix path does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, `normalize('javascript:alert(1)', { reference: 'suffix' })` returns `javascript:alert(1)`, which reparses as a live `javascript:` scheme; `data:` and `vbscript:` behave the same.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that serialize or normalize a relative reference with `reference: 'suffix'` and then treat the result as a URI can turn inert input into a live `javascript:`, `data:`, or `vbscript:` URL (a cross-site scripting vector), and `equal()` can conflate a scheme-bearing reference with a relative one. Exposure is limited to applications that use the non-default `reference: 'suffix'` option.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not use the `reference: 'suffix'` option on untrusted input. If suffix output is required, percent-encode a colon in the first path segment of the result before treating it as a URI.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | fast-uri | 3.1.7 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp | fast-uri | 2.4.8, 3.1.9, 4.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110725\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110725](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-110725)**.\n\n## [GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `fast-uri`'s `serialize()`, `normalize()`, and `resolve()`, when called with the non-default option `reference: 'suffix'` on a reference that has no authority, emit a relative reference whose first path segment contains a bare colon without escaping it. RFC 3986 section 3.3 requires the first segment of a `path-noscheme` reference not to contain a colon, precisely because a bare colon makes the string reparse as an absolute URI with a scheme. `fast-uri`'s default serialization escapes that colon, but the suffix path does not.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, `normalize('javascript:alert(1)', { reference: 'suffix' })` returns `javascript:alert(1)`, which reparses as a live `javascript:` scheme; `data:` and `vbscript:` behave the same.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that serialize or normalize a relative reference with `reference: 'suffix'` and then treat the result as a URI can turn inert input into a live `javascript:`, `data:`, or `vbscript:` URL (a cross-site scripting vector), and `equal()` can conflate a scheme-bearing reference with a relative one. Exposure is limited to applications that use the non-default `reference: 'suffix'` option.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not use the `reference: 'suffix'` option on untrusted input. If suffix output is required, percent-encode a colon in the first path segment of the result before treating it as a URI.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | fast-uri | 3.1.7 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-333x-w4r6-6crp | fast-uri | 2.4.8, 3.1.9, 4.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110725\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
