            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-112943",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112943",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-112943: launch-editor: escapeCmdArgs() omits % escaping on Windows"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Summary\n\n`launch-editor` did not escape `%` on Windows when passing an argument to `cmd`. When an environment variable contained a malicious value, it was possible that that content to be executed.\n\n### Impact\n\nOnly apps that match all of the following conditions are affected:\n\n- The user uses Windows\n- An attacker can place a file with the malicious filename containing `\u0026` or `%`\n- Either of\n  - An attacker can inject an environment variable with a malicious value\n  - An attacker can add a NTFS alternate data stream to the file placed\n\n### Details\n\nUnlike `.sh` files, `.bat` / `.cmd` files on Windows cannot be called directly using [`child_process.spawn`](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v24.x/api/child_process.html#child_processspawncommand-args-options) and requires passing them as an argument of `cmd.exe`. To do so, launch-editor escapes the arguments passed to `cmd.exe`. But `launch-editor` did not escape `%` which is used for environment variables.\n\nBecause `launch-editor` checks if the argument matches an existing filename, the attack requires the attacker to be able to place a file.\n\n- If the attacker can inject an environment variable as well, they can include the subcommand in the env variable (e.g. set ` \u0026calc.exe ` to `%ENV%`) and call `launchEditor` with `something%ENV%`.\n- If the attacker can place a NTFS alternate data stream to the file (e.g. a NTFS alternate data stream named `~-38%calc.exe`), they can call `launchEditor` with `something %CMDCMDLINE:~-38%calc.exe`.\n- If the attacker can create a filename containing the `%CD%` and the subcommand (e.g. `\u0026calc.exe `), they can call `launchEditor` with `./%CD%`",
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\n`launch-editor` did not escape `%` on Windows when passing an argument to `cmd`. When an environment variable contained a malicious value, it was possible that that content to be executed.\n\n### Impact\n\nOnly apps that match all of the following conditions are affected:\n\n- The user uses Windows\n- An attacker can place a file with the malicious filename containing `\u0026` or `%`\n- Either of\n  - An attacker can inject an environment variable with a malicious value\n  - An attacker can add a NTFS alternate data stream to the file placed\n\n### Details\n\nUnlike `.sh` files, `.bat` / `.cmd` files on Windows cannot be called directly using [`child_process.spawn`](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v24.x/api/child_process.html#child_processspawncommand-args-options) and requires passing them as an argument of `cmd.exe`. To do so, launch-editor escapes the arguments passed to `cmd.exe`. But `launch-editor` did not escape `%` which is used for environment variables.\n\nBecause `launch-editor` checks if the argument matches an existing filename, the attack requires the attacker to be able to place a file.\n\n- If the attacker can inject an environment variable as well, they can include the subcommand in the env variable (e.g. set ` \u0026calc.exe ` to `%ENV%`) and call `launchEditor` with `something%ENV%`.\n- If the attacker can place a NTFS alternate data stream to the file (e.g. a NTFS alternate data stream named `~-38%calc.exe`), they can call `launchEditor` with `something %CMDCMDLINE:~-38%calc.exe`.\n- If the attacker can create a filename containing the `%CD%` and the subcommand (e.g. `\u0026calc.exe `), they can call `launchEditor` with `./%CD%`"
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112943",
                "GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112943](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-112943)**.\n\n## [GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `launch-editor` did not escape `%` on Windows when passing an argument to `cmd`. When an environment variable contained a malicious value, it was possible that that content to be executed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only apps that match all of the following conditions are affected:\n\u003e \n\u003e - The user uses Windows\n\u003e - An attacker can place a file with the malicious filename containing `\u0026` or `%`\n\u003e - Either of\n\u003e   - An attacker can inject an environment variable with a malicious value\n\u003e   - An attacker can add a NTFS alternate data stream to the file placed\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e Unlike `.sh` files, `.bat` / `.cmd` files on Windows cannot be called directly using [`child_process.spawn`](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v24.x/api/child_process.html#child_processspawncommand-args-options) and requires passing them as an argument of `cmd.exe`. To do so, launch-editor escapes the arguments passed to `cmd.exe`. But `launch-editor` did not escape `%` which is used for environment variables.\n\u003e \n\u003e Because `launch-editor` checks if the argument matches an existing filename, the attack requires the attacker to be able to place a file.\n\u003e \n\u003e - If the attacker can inject an environment variable as well, they can include the subcommand in the env variable (e.g. set ` \u0026calc.exe ` to `%ENV%`) and call `launchEditor` with `something%ENV%`.\n\u003e - If the attacker can place a NTFS alternate data stream to the file (e.g. a NTFS alternate data stream named `~-38%calc.exe`), they can call `launchEditor` with `something %CMDCMDLINE:~-38%calc.exe`.\n\u003e - If the attacker can create a filename containing the `%CD%` and the subcommand (e.g. `\u0026calc.exe `), they can call `launchEditor` with `./%CD%`\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | vite | 6.4.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv | launch-editor | 2.14.2 |\n| GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv | vite | 6.4.4, 7.3.7, 8.1.6, 8.2.4, 8.3.3 |\n| GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv | vite-plus | 1.1.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112943\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112943](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-112943)**.\n\n## [GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `launch-editor` did not escape `%` on Windows when passing an argument to `cmd`. When an environment variable contained a malicious value, it was possible that that content to be executed.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only apps that match all of the following conditions are affected:\n\u003e \n\u003e - The user uses Windows\n\u003e - An attacker can place a file with the malicious filename containing `\u0026` or `%`\n\u003e - Either of\n\u003e   - An attacker can inject an environment variable with a malicious value\n\u003e   - An attacker can add a NTFS alternate data stream to the file placed\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e Unlike `.sh` files, `.bat` / `.cmd` files on Windows cannot be called directly using [`child_process.spawn`](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v24.x/api/child_process.html#child_processspawncommand-args-options) and requires passing them as an argument of `cmd.exe`. To do so, launch-editor escapes the arguments passed to `cmd.exe`. But `launch-editor` did not escape `%` which is used for environment variables.\n\u003e \n\u003e Because `launch-editor` checks if the argument matches an existing filename, the attack requires the attacker to be able to place a file.\n\u003e \n\u003e - If the attacker can inject an environment variable as well, they can include the subcommand in the env variable (e.g. set ` \u0026calc.exe ` to `%ENV%`) and call `launchEditor` with `something%ENV%`.\n\u003e - If the attacker can place a NTFS alternate data stream to the file (e.g. a NTFS alternate data stream named `~-38%calc.exe`), they can call `launchEditor` with `something %CMDCMDLINE:~-38%calc.exe`.\n\u003e - If the attacker can create a filename containing the `%CD%` and the subcommand (e.g. `\u0026calc.exe `), they can call `launchEditor` with `./%CD%`\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | vite | 6.4.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv | launch-editor | 2.14.2 |\n| GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv | vite | 6.4.4, 7.3.7, 8.1.6, 8.2.4, 8.3.3 |\n| GHSA-698v-4r3x-3vcv | vite-plus | 1.1.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112943\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-110816",
              "name": "CVE-2026-110816",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-110816: qs : qs.stringify with arrayFormat 'comma' throws an uncaught TypeError on array elements carrying a non-callable own toString (DoS)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "## Summary\n\n`qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })` throws an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array in `obj` contains an element that carries an own, non-callable `toString` property (for example `{ a: [ { toString: 'x' } ] }`). A remote, unauthenticated attacker who can influence an object that the application later re-serializes with the comma array format triggers the throw. In a service that lacks a synchronous error boundary around the serialization call, the uncaught exception crashes the worker process, producing a denial of service.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nWhen `arrayFormat: 'comma'` is selected, the comma branch collapses the whole array into a single value via `Array.prototype.join`, or, under `encodeValuesOnly: true`, maps each element through the value encoder before joining. Both paths perform an implicit string coercion (`ToPrimitive` with hint `\"string\"`) on each element. For an object whose own `toString` is a non-callable value, coercion skips the shadowed non-callable `toString`, falls through to `Object.prototype.valueOf` (which returns the object rather than a primitive), and throws. The comma branch runs this coercion before the per-element null and skip handling in the main loop, so nothing downstream can neutralize the hostile element, and the throw is never caught inside the library.\n\n```text\nlib/stringify.js  (comma-array branch)\n  line 152-154: obj = utils.maybeMap(obj, function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); });  // encodeValuesOnly path\n  line 156:     objKeys = [{ value: obj.length \u003e 0 ? obj.join(',') || null : void undefined }];   // default comma path\nlib/utils.js\n  line 292:     string = String(str);   // value encoder coercion reached via the encodeValuesOnly path\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nPrerequisites: the application calls `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` (a documented, supported option) on an object whose shape can be influenced by attacker input. Attacker-controllable sources include `qs.parse(input, { allowPrototypes: true })`, which retains a `toString` segment (Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set it), `qs.parse(input, { plainObjects: true })`, whose null-prototype elements throw even without a `toString` key (eg, `a[][b]=c`), and `JSON.parse` of a request body, which needs no qs parse option at all. No authentication or user interaction is required. Demonstrated capability: a single request forces an uncaught synchronous `TypeError` in the serialization call. In a naive single-process Node service with no synchronous error boundary, this crashes the process and takes the service offline (availability impact). Under a framework such as Express that traps synchronous handler throws, the plausible outcome is a per-request 500 rather than a full outage; that lower-impact case is noted but not the worst credible supported deployment. There is no confidentiality or integrity impact, no memory unsafety, and no prototype pollution.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n`Install qs at the affected version (for example 6.16.0) and run the following script with Node.js. No network setup is required; it demonstrates the throw in isolation.`\n\n```js\nvar qs = require('qs');\n\n// Source A: qs.parse with allowPrototypes retains the hostile toString segment\nqs.stringify(qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x', { allowPrototypes: true }), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Source B: a JSON request body, no qs parse option needed\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Also affected: the encodeValuesOnly sub-path\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma', encodeValuesOnly: true });\n```\n\n```text\nA default-join (allowPrototypes) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nB default-join (JSON.parse) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nC encodeValuesOnly path =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nN2 callable toString =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a=z\"\nN3 isBuffer vector (CVE-2026-82417) =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a%5Bconstructor%5D%5BisBuffer%5D=x\"\nqs version: 6.16.0\n```\n\n## Attack Chain\n\n1. **Exposure:** A remote, unauthenticated request reaches an endpoint whose handler eventually serializes attacker-influenced data with `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })`.\n2. **Control:** The attacker supplies data that becomes `{ a: [ { toString: \u003cnon-callable value\u003e } ] }`, either via `a[0][toString]=x` parsed with `allowPrototypes`, or via a JSON body `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n3. **Path:** In `stringify`, the primitive/buffer guard at `lib/stringify.js:134` is false for an array, so control reaches the comma branch at `lib/stringify.js:149`.\n4. **Guard:** On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, the closure `v == null ? v : encoder(v)` at `lib/stringify.js:152-154` passes only `null`/`undefined` through; a non-null object still reaches the encoder. The default path skips that closure entirely and reaches `obj.join(',')` at `lib/stringify.js:156` with no guard at all.\n5. **Primitive:** The element is coerced to a string (`Array.prototype.join`, or `String(str)` at `lib/utils.js:292`). `ToPrimitive` skips the non-callable own `toString`, `valueOf` returns the object, and a `TypeError` is thrown.\n6. **Result:** The uncaught synchronous exception propagates out of the serialization call, failing the request and, in a service without a synchronous error boundary, crashing the process (denial of service).\n\n## Bypass Evidence\n\nThis is a different element type on the code path CVE-2026-8723 (GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26) patched, plus the default comma path, which that fix never covered. CVE-2026-8723 addressed a `TypeError` crash on `null`/`undefined` entries in the comma plus `encodeValuesOnly` path. Its fix (commit 21f80b3) wraps the encoder in `function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); }`, so it only passes `null` and `undefined` through. A non-null element with a hostile own `toString` fails the `v == null` test, so the encoder still runs and still throws; the encodeValuesOnly case (case C above) throws on the exact fixed code. The default comma path (`encodeValuesOnly` unset, the library default) never entered that fix at all: `git log -S \"obj.join(','\" -- lib/stringify.js` shows no security fix has ever touched the `obj.join(',')` line, and cases A and B throw with `encodeValuesOnly` unset. The related isBuffer hardening for CVE-2026-82417 (commit e83d321) is a different sink at the `lib/stringify.js:134` guard, which is never entered for the array element; the negative control `{a:{constructor:{isBuffer:'x'}}}` serializes cleanly. Disproof attempts that failed: a `valueOf`-only object does not throw (it serializes to `[object Object]`), confirming the trigger is an element with no callable `toString` (an own non-callable `toString`, or a null-prototype object with no `toString` at all, as `qs.parse` produces under `plainObjects`); nested arrays (`{\"a\":[[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]]}`) throw the same way; a callable `toString` serializes cleanly; and the default (non-comma) array format serializes cleanly, confirming the comma format is required. All positive and negative cases were reproduced on the exact published 6.16.0 artifact.\n\n## Affected Versions\n\n- `Ecosystem: npm`\n- `Package: qs`\n- `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0`\n- `Latest release checked: 6.16.0 (npm latest; git tag v6.16.0)`\n- `Fix status: fixed in 6.16.1 (a952e75)`\n\nThe `arrayFormat: 'comma'` join sink was introduced in qs 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 is the lower boundary; releases before 6.7.0 lack the sink. No released version fixes this vector, and the latest published release (6.16.0) reproduces the throw, so the closed upper bound `\u003c= 6.16.0` covers every affected release. The `encodeValuesOnly` sub-path was added later, but the default comma-join vector already affects 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 governs the lower boundary.\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nThe comma array serialization must not allow an implicit string coercion of an array element to throw an uncaught exception. Coercion of each comma element should be performed defensively (for example by wrapping the join and per-element encode so that a coercion failure is caught and handled), and this protection must cover both the default `obj.join(',')` path and the `encodeValuesOnly` encoder path, not only the previously patched `null`/`undefined` case. As an interim mitigation without a code change, applications can avoid `arrayFormat: 'comma'` when serializing data derived from untrusted input, sanitize parsed objects (avoid `allowPrototypes`/`plainObjects` on untrusted input and strip unexpected own properties), or wrap `qs.stringify` calls in a synchronous error boundary so a coercion failure cannot crash the process.\n\nReported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\n---\n\n## Independent report by @Lucidsec\n\nThe following was reported separately, after this advisory, and is merged here as a duplicate.\n\n### Summary\n\nIn project [qs](https://github.com/ljharb/qs), in versions `\u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0` (every version that supports `arrayFormat: 'comma'`), `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` can throw an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array element has a non-callable own `toString`. It is fixed in 6.16.1. Related: CWE-248 and CWE-704.\n\n### Technical Details\n\n`qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` joins an array at `lib/stringify.js:156` via `obj.join(',')`. An element whose own `toString` is not callable makes `Array.prototype.join` throw. There is no type check and no catch on that path.\n\nA second path exists with `encodeValuesOnly`: at `lib/stringify.js:151-154`, `maybeMap` only skips `v == null`, then `lib/utils.js:292` does `String(str)` and throws the same `TypeError` (this second site exists since v6.11.1; before that, the same input throws at `join`).\n\nWho can trigger: someone who can shape an array element passed to `stringify`. The host must use non-default `arrayFormat: 'comma'`. Default `arrayFormat` `indices` does not fold arrays and is not affected. A parse-then-stringify path also needs `plainObjects: true` (null-prototype elements from `lib/parse.js:200`) or `allowPrototypes: true` (which Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set). Default `qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x')` returns `{}` because the prototype guard drops `toString`. Direct JSON handed to `stringify` can supply `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\nCause: the comma fold at `lib/stringify.js:149-156` collapses the array into a single value before the main loop at `lib/stringify.js:172-212`, so `skipNulls` and `strictNullHandling` only ever see the joined value, never individual elements. `isNonNullishPrimitive` / `isBuffer` at `lib/stringify.js:134` inspect the whole value, not each element. A normal object uses `Object.prototype.toString` and becomes `[object Object]`. An own non-callable `toString` is skipped, `valueOf` returns the object, and `join` throws. (The skipped TODO test at `test/stringify.js:684-689` uses a plain `{ b: 1 }` element, which does not throw; it covers the separate question of what comma format should output for object elements, not this crash.) Earlier comma crash fixes did not cover object elements.\n\n### Impact\n\nOne small input makes that serialization path throw a synchronous, catchable `TypeError`. Behind a framework error boundary (eg, a synchronous Express handler), the request fails with a 500 and the process survives. In a host with no error boundary (eg, a bare `http.createServer` handler), the uncaught exception exits the process (the client sees `ECONNRESET`). Availability only.\n\n### Proof of Concept\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eShow the proof of concept\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nChecked on the latest commit that was checked: `07b1d4d82c8f9301c105ea4b94fa2302cfd6e8b4`. Still present. Demonstrated.\n\n- Payload `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}` throws at `lib/stringify.js:156`.\n- Baseline `{\"a\":[\"x\",\"y\"]}` returns 200 with body `a=x%2Cy`.\n- A plain object element stringifies to `a=%5Bobject%20Object%5D` and does not throw.\n- `encodeValuesOnly: true` throws via `utils.js:292`.\n- `plainObjects` parse plus comma throws.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n### Suggested Fix\n\nBefore `join`, map elements with the existing primitive check; skip or replace non-primitives so `join` cannot throw. On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, do not send non-primitives into `String()`. Optionally guard `lib/utils.js:292`. Add new regression tests.",
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\n`qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })` throws an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array in `obj` contains an element that carries an own, non-callable `toString` property (for example `{ a: [ { toString: 'x' } ] }`). A remote, unauthenticated attacker who can influence an object that the application later re-serializes with the comma array format triggers the throw. In a service that lacks a synchronous error boundary around the serialization call, the uncaught exception crashes the worker process, producing a denial of service.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nWhen `arrayFormat: 'comma'` is selected, the comma branch collapses the whole array into a single value via `Array.prototype.join`, or, under `encodeValuesOnly: true`, maps each element through the value encoder before joining. Both paths perform an implicit string coercion (`ToPrimitive` with hint `\"string\"`) on each element. For an object whose own `toString` is a non-callable value, coercion skips the shadowed non-callable `toString`, falls through to `Object.prototype.valueOf` (which returns the object rather than a primitive), and throws. The comma branch runs this coercion before the per-element null and skip handling in the main loop, so nothing downstream can neutralize the hostile element, and the throw is never caught inside the library.\n\n```text\nlib/stringify.js  (comma-array branch)\n  line 152-154: obj = utils.maybeMap(obj, function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); });  // encodeValuesOnly path\n  line 156:     objKeys = [{ value: obj.length \u003e 0 ? obj.join(',') || null : void undefined }];   // default comma path\nlib/utils.js\n  line 292:     string = String(str);   // value encoder coercion reached via the encodeValuesOnly path\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nPrerequisites: the application calls `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` (a documented, supported option) on an object whose shape can be influenced by attacker input. Attacker-controllable sources include `qs.parse(input, { allowPrototypes: true })`, which retains a `toString` segment (Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set it), `qs.parse(input, { plainObjects: true })`, whose null-prototype elements throw even without a `toString` key (eg, `a[][b]=c`), and `JSON.parse` of a request body, which needs no qs parse option at all. No authentication or user interaction is required. Demonstrated capability: a single request forces an uncaught synchronous `TypeError` in the serialization call. In a naive single-process Node service with no synchronous error boundary, this crashes the process and takes the service offline (availability impact). Under a framework such as Express that traps synchronous handler throws, the plausible outcome is a per-request 500 rather than a full outage; that lower-impact case is noted but not the worst credible supported deployment. There is no confidentiality or integrity impact, no memory unsafety, and no prototype pollution.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\n`Install qs at the affected version (for example 6.16.0) and run the following script with Node.js. No network setup is required; it demonstrates the throw in isolation.`\n\n```js\nvar qs = require('qs');\n\n// Source A: qs.parse with allowPrototypes retains the hostile toString segment\nqs.stringify(qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x', { allowPrototypes: true }), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Source B: a JSON request body, no qs parse option needed\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\n// Also affected: the encodeValuesOnly sub-path\nqs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma', encodeValuesOnly: true });\n```\n\n```text\nA default-join (allowPrototypes) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nB default-join (JSON.parse) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nC encodeValuesOnly path =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\nN2 callable toString =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a=z\"\nN3 isBuffer vector (CVE-2026-82417) =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a%5Bconstructor%5D%5BisBuffer%5D=x\"\nqs version: 6.16.0\n```\n\n## Attack Chain\n\n1. **Exposure:** A remote, unauthenticated request reaches an endpoint whose handler eventually serializes attacker-influenced data with `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })`.\n2. **Control:** The attacker supplies data that becomes `{ a: [ { toString: \u003cnon-callable value\u003e } ] }`, either via `a[0][toString]=x` parsed with `allowPrototypes`, or via a JSON body `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n3. **Path:** In `stringify`, the primitive/buffer guard at `lib/stringify.js:134` is false for an array, so control reaches the comma branch at `lib/stringify.js:149`.\n4. **Guard:** On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, the closure `v == null ? v : encoder(v)` at `lib/stringify.js:152-154` passes only `null`/`undefined` through; a non-null object still reaches the encoder. The default path skips that closure entirely and reaches `obj.join(',')` at `lib/stringify.js:156` with no guard at all.\n5. **Primitive:** The element is coerced to a string (`Array.prototype.join`, or `String(str)` at `lib/utils.js:292`). `ToPrimitive` skips the non-callable own `toString`, `valueOf` returns the object, and a `TypeError` is thrown.\n6. **Result:** The uncaught synchronous exception propagates out of the serialization call, failing the request and, in a service without a synchronous error boundary, crashing the process (denial of service).\n\n## Bypass Evidence\n\nThis is a different element type on the code path CVE-2026-8723 (GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26) patched, plus the default comma path, which that fix never covered. CVE-2026-8723 addressed a `TypeError` crash on `null`/`undefined` entries in the comma plus `encodeValuesOnly` path. Its fix (commit 21f80b3) wraps the encoder in `function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); }`, so it only passes `null` and `undefined` through. A non-null element with a hostile own `toString` fails the `v == null` test, so the encoder still runs and still throws; the encodeValuesOnly case (case C above) throws on the exact fixed code. The default comma path (`encodeValuesOnly` unset, the library default) never entered that fix at all: `git log -S \"obj.join(','\" -- lib/stringify.js` shows no security fix has ever touched the `obj.join(',')` line, and cases A and B throw with `encodeValuesOnly` unset. The related isBuffer hardening for CVE-2026-82417 (commit e83d321) is a different sink at the `lib/stringify.js:134` guard, which is never entered for the array element; the negative control `{a:{constructor:{isBuffer:'x'}}}` serializes cleanly. Disproof attempts that failed: a `valueOf`-only object does not throw (it serializes to `[object Object]`), confirming the trigger is an element with no callable `toString` (an own non-callable `toString`, or a null-prototype object with no `toString` at all, as `qs.parse` produces under `plainObjects`); nested arrays (`{\"a\":[[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]]}`) throw the same way; a callable `toString` serializes cleanly; and the default (non-comma) array format serializes cleanly, confirming the comma format is required. All positive and negative cases were reproduced on the exact published 6.16.0 artifact.\n\n## Affected Versions\n\n- `Ecosystem: npm`\n- `Package: qs`\n- `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0`\n- `Latest release checked: 6.16.0 (npm latest; git tag v6.16.0)`\n- `Fix status: fixed in 6.16.1 (a952e75)`\n\nThe `arrayFormat: 'comma'` join sink was introduced in qs 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 is the lower boundary; releases before 6.7.0 lack the sink. No released version fixes this vector, and the latest published release (6.16.0) reproduces the throw, so the closed upper bound `\u003c= 6.16.0` covers every affected release. The `encodeValuesOnly` sub-path was added later, but the default comma-join vector already affects 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 governs the lower boundary.\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nThe comma array serialization must not allow an implicit string coercion of an array element to throw an uncaught exception. Coercion of each comma element should be performed defensively (for example by wrapping the join and per-element encode so that a coercion failure is caught and handled), and this protection must cover both the default `obj.join(',')` path and the `encodeValuesOnly` encoder path, not only the previously patched `null`/`undefined` case. As an interim mitigation without a code change, applications can avoid `arrayFormat: 'comma'` when serializing data derived from untrusted input, sanitize parsed objects (avoid `allowPrototypes`/`plainObjects` on untrusted input and strip unexpected own properties), or wrap `qs.stringify` calls in a synchronous error boundary so a coercion failure cannot crash the process.\n\nReported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\n---\n\n## Independent report by @Lucidsec\n\nThe following was reported separately, after this advisory, and is merged here as a duplicate.\n\n### Summary\n\nIn project [qs](https://github.com/ljharb/qs), in versions `\u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0` (every version that supports `arrayFormat: 'comma'`), `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` can throw an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array element has a non-callable own `toString`. It is fixed in 6.16.1. Related: CWE-248 and CWE-704.\n\n### Technical Details\n\n`qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` joins an array at `lib/stringify.js:156` via `obj.join(',')`. An element whose own `toString` is not callable makes `Array.prototype.join` throw. There is no type check and no catch on that path.\n\nA second path exists with `encodeValuesOnly`: at `lib/stringify.js:151-154`, `maybeMap` only skips `v == null`, then `lib/utils.js:292` does `String(str)` and throws the same `TypeError` (this second site exists since v6.11.1; before that, the same input throws at `join`).\n\nWho can trigger: someone who can shape an array element passed to `stringify`. The host must use non-default `arrayFormat: 'comma'`. Default `arrayFormat` `indices` does not fold arrays and is not affected. A parse-then-stringify path also needs `plainObjects: true` (null-prototype elements from `lib/parse.js:200`) or `allowPrototypes: true` (which Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set). Default `qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x')` returns `{}` because the prototype guard drops `toString`. Direct JSON handed to `stringify` can supply `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\nCause: the comma fold at `lib/stringify.js:149-156` collapses the array into a single value before the main loop at `lib/stringify.js:172-212`, so `skipNulls` and `strictNullHandling` only ever see the joined value, never individual elements. `isNonNullishPrimitive` / `isBuffer` at `lib/stringify.js:134` inspect the whole value, not each element. A normal object uses `Object.prototype.toString` and becomes `[object Object]`. An own non-callable `toString` is skipped, `valueOf` returns the object, and `join` throws. (The skipped TODO test at `test/stringify.js:684-689` uses a plain `{ b: 1 }` element, which does not throw; it covers the separate question of what comma format should output for object elements, not this crash.) Earlier comma crash fixes did not cover object elements.\n\n### Impact\n\nOne small input makes that serialization path throw a synchronous, catchable `TypeError`. Behind a framework error boundary (eg, a synchronous Express handler), the request fails with a 500 and the process survives. In a host with no error boundary (eg, a bare `http.createServer` handler), the uncaught exception exits the process (the client sees `ECONNRESET`). Availability only.\n\n### Proof of Concept\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eShow the proof of concept\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nChecked on the latest commit that was checked: `07b1d4d82c8f9301c105ea4b94fa2302cfd6e8b4`. Still present. Demonstrated.\n\n- Payload `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}` throws at `lib/stringify.js:156`.\n- Baseline `{\"a\":[\"x\",\"y\"]}` returns 200 with body `a=x%2Cy`.\n- A plain object element stringifies to `a=%5Bobject%20Object%5D` and does not throw.\n- `encodeValuesOnly: true` throws via `utils.js:292`.\n- `plainObjects` parse plus comma throws.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n### Suggested Fix\n\nBefore `join`, map elements with the existing primitive check; skip or replace non-primitives so `join` cannot throw. On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, do not send non-primitives into `String()`. Optionally guard `lib/utils.js:292`. Add new regression tests."
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110816",
                "GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110816](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-110816)**.\n\n## [GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })` throws an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array in `obj` contains an element that carries an own, non-callable `toString` property (for example `{ a: [ { toString: 'x' } ] }`). A remote, unauthenticated attacker who can influence an object that the application later re-serializes with the comma array format triggers the throw. In a service that lacks a synchronous error boundary around the serialization call, the uncaught exception crashes the worker process, producing a denial of service.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Root Cause\n\u003e \n\u003e When `arrayFormat: 'comma'` is selected, the comma branch collapses the whole array into a single value via `Array.prototype.join`, or, under `encodeValuesOnly: true`, maps each element through the value encoder before joining. Both paths perform an implicit string coercion (`ToPrimitive` with hint `\"string\"`) on each element. For an object whose own `toString` is a non-callable value, coercion skips the shadowed non-callable `toString`, falls through to `Object.prototype.valueOf` (which returns the object rather than a primitive), and throws. The comma branch runs this coercion before the per-element null and skip handling in the main loop, so nothing downstream can neutralize the hostile element, and the throw is never caught inside the library.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e lib/stringify.js  (comma-array branch)\n\u003e   line 152-154: obj = utils.maybeMap(obj, function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); });  // encodeValuesOnly path\n\u003e   line 156:     objKeys = [{ value: obj.length \u003e 0 ? obj.join(',') || null : void undefined }];   // default comma path\n\u003e lib/utils.js\n\u003e   line 292:     string = String(str);   // value encoder coercion reached via the encodeValuesOnly path\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Prerequisites: the application calls `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` (a documented, supported option) on an object whose shape can be influenced by attacker input. Attacker-controllable sources include `qs.parse(input, { allowPrototypes: true })`, which retains a `toString` segment (Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set it), `qs.parse(input, { plainObjects: true })`, whose null-prototype elements throw even without a `toString` key (eg, `a[][b]=c`), and `JSON.parse` of a request body, which needs no qs parse option at all. No authentication or user interaction is required. Demonstrated capability: a single request forces an uncaught synchronous `TypeError` in the serialization call. In a naive single-process Node service with no synchronous error boundary, this crashes the process and takes the service offline (availability impact). Under a framework such as Express that traps synchronous handler throws, the plausible outcome is a per-request 500 rather than a full outage; that lower-impact case is noted but not the worst credible supported deployment. There is no confidentiality or integrity impact, no memory unsafety, and no prototype pollution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e `Install qs at the affected version (for example 6.16.0) and run the following script with Node.js. No network setup is required; it demonstrates the throw in isolation.`\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var qs = require('qs');\n\u003e \n\u003e // Source A: qs.parse with allowPrototypes retains the hostile toString segment\n\u003e qs.stringify(qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x', { allowPrototypes: true }), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\u003e \n\u003e // Source B: a JSON request body, no qs parse option needed\n\u003e qs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\u003e \n\u003e // Also affected: the encodeValuesOnly sub-path\n\u003e qs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma', encodeValuesOnly: true });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e A default-join (allowPrototypes) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\n\u003e B default-join (JSON.parse) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\n\u003e C encodeValuesOnly path =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\n\u003e N2 callable toString =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a=z\"\n\u003e N3 isBuffer vector (CVE-2026-82417) =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a%5Bconstructor%5D%5BisBuffer%5D=x\"\n\u003e qs version: 6.16.0\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack Chain\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **Exposure:** A remote, unauthenticated request reaches an endpoint whose handler eventually serializes attacker-influenced data with `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })`.\n\u003e 2. **Control:** The attacker supplies data that becomes `{ a: [ { toString: \u003cnon-callable value\u003e } ] }`, either via `a[0][toString]=x` parsed with `allowPrototypes`, or via a JSON body `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\u003e 3. **Path:** In `stringify`, the primitive/buffer guard at `lib/stringify.js:134` is false for an array, so control reaches the comma branch at `lib/stringify.js:149`.\n\u003e 4. **Guard:** On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, the closure `v == null ? v : encoder(v)` at `lib/stringify.js:152-154` passes only `null`/`undefined` through; a non-null object still reaches the encoder. The default path skips that closure entirely and reaches `obj.join(',')` at `lib/stringify.js:156` with no guard at all.\n\u003e 5. **Primitive:** The element is coerced to a string (`Array.prototype.join`, or `String(str)` at `lib/utils.js:292`). `ToPrimitive` skips the non-callable own `toString`, `valueOf` returns the object, and a `TypeError` is thrown.\n\u003e 6. **Result:** The uncaught synchronous exception propagates out of the serialization call, failing the request and, in a service without a synchronous error boundary, crashing the process (denial of service).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Bypass Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a different element type on the code path CVE-2026-8723 (GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26) patched, plus the default comma path, which that fix never covered. CVE-2026-8723 addressed a `TypeError` crash on `null`/`undefined` entries in the comma plus `encodeValuesOnly` path. Its fix (commit 21f80b3) wraps the encoder in `function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); }`, so it only passes `null` and `undefined` through. A non-null element with a hostile own `toString` fails the `v == null` test, so the encoder still runs and still throws; the encodeValuesOnly case (case C above) throws on the exact fixed code. The default comma path (`encodeValuesOnly` unset, the library default) never entered that fix at all: `git log -S \"obj.join(','\" -- lib/stringify.js` shows no security fix has ever touched the `obj.join(',')` line, and cases A and B throw with `encodeValuesOnly` unset. The related isBuffer hardening for CVE-2026-82417 (commit e83d321) is a different sink at the `lib/stringify.js:134` guard, which is never entered for the array element; the negative control `{a:{constructor:{isBuffer:'x'}}}` serializes cleanly. Disproof attempts that failed: a `valueOf`-only object does not throw (it serializes to `[object Object]`), confirming the trigger is an element with no callable `toString` (an own non-callable `toString`, or a null-prototype object with no `toString` at all, as `qs.parse` produces under `plainObjects`); nested arrays (`{\"a\":[[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]]}`) throw the same way; a callable `toString` serializes cleanly; and the default (non-comma) array format serializes cleanly, confirming the comma format is required. All positive and negative cases were reproduced on the exact published 6.16.0 artifact.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected Versions\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Ecosystem: npm`\n\u003e - `Package: qs`\n\u003e - `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0`\n\u003e - `Latest release checked: 6.16.0 (npm latest; git tag v6.16.0)`\n\u003e - `Fix status: fixed in 6.16.1 (a952e75)`\n\u003e \n\u003e The `arrayFormat: 'comma'` join sink was introduced in qs 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 is the lower boundary; releases before 6.7.0 lack the sink. No released version fixes this vector, and the latest published release (6.16.0) reproduces the throw, so the closed upper bound `\u003c= 6.16.0` covers every affected release. The `encodeValuesOnly` sub-path was added later, but the default comma-join vector already affects 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 governs the lower boundary.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested Fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The comma array serialization must not allow an implicit string coercion of an array element to throw an uncaught exception. Coercion of each comma element should be performed defensively (for example by wrapping the join and per-element encode so that a coercion failure is caught and handled), and this protection must cover both the default `obj.join(',')` path and the `encodeValuesOnly` encoder path, not only the previously patched `null`/`undefined` case. As an interim mitigation without a code change, applications can avoid `arrayFormat: 'comma'` when serializing data derived from untrusted input, sanitize parsed objects (avoid `allowPrototypes`/`plainObjects` on untrusted input and strip unexpected own properties), or wrap `qs.stringify` calls in a synchronous error boundary so a coercion failure cannot crash the process.\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Independent report by @Lucidsec\n\u003e \n\u003e The following was reported separately, after this advisory, and is merged here as a duplicate.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e In project [qs](https://github.com/ljharb/qs), in versions `\u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0` (every version that supports `arrayFormat: 'comma'`), `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` can throw an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array element has a non-callable own `toString`. It is fixed in 6.16.1. Related: CWE-248 and CWE-704.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Technical Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` joins an array at `lib/stringify.js:156` via `obj.join(',')`. An element whose own `toString` is not callable makes `Array.prototype.join` throw. There is no type check and no catch on that path.\n\u003e \n\u003e A second path exists with `encodeValuesOnly`: at `lib/stringify.js:151-154`, `maybeMap` only skips `v == null`, then `lib/utils.js:292` does `String(str)` and throws the same `TypeError` (this second site exists since v6.11.1; before that, the same input throws at `join`).\n\u003e \n\u003e Who can trigger: someone who can shape an array element passed to `stringify`. The host must use non-default `arrayFormat: 'comma'`. Default `arrayFormat` `indices` does not fold arrays and is not affected. A parse-then-stringify path also needs `plainObjects: true` (null-prototype elements from `lib/parse.js:200`) or `allowPrototypes: true` (which Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set). Default `qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x')` returns `{}` because the prototype guard drops `toString`. Direct JSON handed to `stringify` can supply `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Cause: the comma fold at `lib/stringify.js:149-156` collapses the array into a single value before the main loop at `lib/stringify.js:172-212`, so `skipNulls` and `strictNullHandling` only ever see the joined value, never individual elements. `isNonNullishPrimitive` / `isBuffer` at `lib/stringify.js:134` inspect the whole value, not each element. A normal object uses `Object.prototype.toString` and becomes `[object Object]`. An own non-callable `toString` is skipped, `valueOf` returns the object, and `join` throws. (The skipped TODO test at `test/stringify.js:684-689` uses a plain `{ b: 1 }` element, which does not throw; it covers the separate question of what comma format should output for object elements, not this crash.) Earlier comma crash fixes did not cover object elements.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e One small input makes that serialization path throw a synchronous, catchable `TypeError`. Behind a framework error boundary (eg, a synchronous Express handler), the request fails with a 500 and the process survives. In a host with no error boundary (eg, a bare `http.createServer` handler), the uncaught exception exits the process (the client sees `ECONNRESET`). Availability only.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eShow the proof of concept\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Checked on the latest commit that was checked: `07b1d4d82c8f9301c105ea4b94fa2302cfd6e8b4`. Still present. Demonstrated.\n\u003e \n\u003e - Payload `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}` throws at `lib/stringify.js:156`.\n\u003e - Baseline `{\"a\":[\"x\",\"y\"]}` returns 200 with body `a=x%2Cy`.\n\u003e - A plain object element stringifies to `a=%5Bobject%20Object%5D` and does not throw.\n\u003e - `encodeValuesOnly: true` throws via `utils.js:292`.\n\u003e - `plainObjects` parse plus comma throws.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested Fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Before `join`, map elements with the existing primitive check; skip or replace non-primitives so `join` cannot throw. On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, do not send non-primitives into `String()`. Optionally guard `lib/utils.js:292`. Add new regression tests.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | qs | 6.16.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw | qs | 6.16.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110816\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110816](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-110816)**.\n\n## [GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })` throws an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array in `obj` contains an element that carries an own, non-callable `toString` property (for example `{ a: [ { toString: 'x' } ] }`). A remote, unauthenticated attacker who can influence an object that the application later re-serializes with the comma array format triggers the throw. In a service that lacks a synchronous error boundary around the serialization call, the uncaught exception crashes the worker process, producing a denial of service.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Root Cause\n\u003e \n\u003e When `arrayFormat: 'comma'` is selected, the comma branch collapses the whole array into a single value via `Array.prototype.join`, or, under `encodeValuesOnly: true`, maps each element through the value encoder before joining. Both paths perform an implicit string coercion (`ToPrimitive` with hint `\"string\"`) on each element. For an object whose own `toString` is a non-callable value, coercion skips the shadowed non-callable `toString`, falls through to `Object.prototype.valueOf` (which returns the object rather than a primitive), and throws. The comma branch runs this coercion before the per-element null and skip handling in the main loop, so nothing downstream can neutralize the hostile element, and the throw is never caught inside the library.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e lib/stringify.js  (comma-array branch)\n\u003e   line 152-154: obj = utils.maybeMap(obj, function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); });  // encodeValuesOnly path\n\u003e   line 156:     objKeys = [{ value: obj.length \u003e 0 ? obj.join(',') || null : void undefined }];   // default comma path\n\u003e lib/utils.js\n\u003e   line 292:     string = String(str);   // value encoder coercion reached via the encodeValuesOnly path\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Prerequisites: the application calls `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` (a documented, supported option) on an object whose shape can be influenced by attacker input. Attacker-controllable sources include `qs.parse(input, { allowPrototypes: true })`, which retains a `toString` segment (Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set it), `qs.parse(input, { plainObjects: true })`, whose null-prototype elements throw even without a `toString` key (eg, `a[][b]=c`), and `JSON.parse` of a request body, which needs no qs parse option at all. No authentication or user interaction is required. Demonstrated capability: a single request forces an uncaught synchronous `TypeError` in the serialization call. In a naive single-process Node service with no synchronous error boundary, this crashes the process and takes the service offline (availability impact). Under a framework such as Express that traps synchronous handler throws, the plausible outcome is a per-request 500 rather than a full outage; that lower-impact case is noted but not the worst credible supported deployment. There is no confidentiality or integrity impact, no memory unsafety, and no prototype pollution.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e `Install qs at the affected version (for example 6.16.0) and run the following script with Node.js. No network setup is required; it demonstrates the throw in isolation.`\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e var qs = require('qs');\n\u003e \n\u003e // Source A: qs.parse with allowPrototypes retains the hostile toString segment\n\u003e qs.stringify(qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x', { allowPrototypes: true }), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\u003e \n\u003e // Source B: a JSON request body, no qs parse option needed\n\u003e qs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma' });\n\u003e \n\u003e // Also affected: the encodeValuesOnly sub-path\n\u003e qs.stringify(JSON.parse('{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}'), { arrayFormat: 'comma', encodeValuesOnly: true });\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e A default-join (allowPrototypes) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\n\u003e B default-join (JSON.parse) =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\n\u003e C encodeValuesOnly path =\u003e THREW: TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value\n\u003e N2 callable toString =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a=z\"\n\u003e N3 isBuffer vector (CVE-2026-82417) =\u003e NO THROW; output=\"a%5Bconstructor%5D%5BisBuffer%5D=x\"\n\u003e qs version: 6.16.0\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Attack Chain\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. **Exposure:** A remote, unauthenticated request reaches an endpoint whose handler eventually serializes attacker-influenced data with `qs.stringify(obj, { arrayFormat: 'comma' })`.\n\u003e 2. **Control:** The attacker supplies data that becomes `{ a: [ { toString: \u003cnon-callable value\u003e } ] }`, either via `a[0][toString]=x` parsed with `allowPrototypes`, or via a JSON body `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\u003e 3. **Path:** In `stringify`, the primitive/buffer guard at `lib/stringify.js:134` is false for an array, so control reaches the comma branch at `lib/stringify.js:149`.\n\u003e 4. **Guard:** On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, the closure `v == null ? v : encoder(v)` at `lib/stringify.js:152-154` passes only `null`/`undefined` through; a non-null object still reaches the encoder. The default path skips that closure entirely and reaches `obj.join(',')` at `lib/stringify.js:156` with no guard at all.\n\u003e 5. **Primitive:** The element is coerced to a string (`Array.prototype.join`, or `String(str)` at `lib/utils.js:292`). `ToPrimitive` skips the non-callable own `toString`, `valueOf` returns the object, and a `TypeError` is thrown.\n\u003e 6. **Result:** The uncaught synchronous exception propagates out of the serialization call, failing the request and, in a service without a synchronous error boundary, crashing the process (denial of service).\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Bypass Evidence\n\u003e \n\u003e This is a different element type on the code path CVE-2026-8723 (GHSA-q8mj-m7cp-5q26) patched, plus the default comma path, which that fix never covered. CVE-2026-8723 addressed a `TypeError` crash on `null`/`undefined` entries in the comma plus `encodeValuesOnly` path. Its fix (commit 21f80b3) wraps the encoder in `function (v) { return v == null ? v : encoder(v); }`, so it only passes `null` and `undefined` through. A non-null element with a hostile own `toString` fails the `v == null` test, so the encoder still runs and still throws; the encodeValuesOnly case (case C above) throws on the exact fixed code. The default comma path (`encodeValuesOnly` unset, the library default) never entered that fix at all: `git log -S \"obj.join(','\" -- lib/stringify.js` shows no security fix has ever touched the `obj.join(',')` line, and cases A and B throw with `encodeValuesOnly` unset. The related isBuffer hardening for CVE-2026-82417 (commit e83d321) is a different sink at the `lib/stringify.js:134` guard, which is never entered for the array element; the negative control `{a:{constructor:{isBuffer:'x'}}}` serializes cleanly. Disproof attempts that failed: a `valueOf`-only object does not throw (it serializes to `[object Object]`), confirming the trigger is an element with no callable `toString` (an own non-callable `toString`, or a null-prototype object with no `toString` at all, as `qs.parse` produces under `plainObjects`); nested arrays (`{\"a\":[[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]]}`) throw the same way; a callable `toString` serializes cleanly; and the default (non-comma) array format serializes cleanly, confirming the comma format is required. All positive and negative cases were reproduced on the exact published 6.16.0 artifact.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Affected Versions\n\u003e \n\u003e - `Ecosystem: npm`\n\u003e - `Package: qs`\n\u003e - `Confirmed affected range: \u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0`\n\u003e - `Latest release checked: 6.16.0 (npm latest; git tag v6.16.0)`\n\u003e - `Fix status: fixed in 6.16.1 (a952e75)`\n\u003e \n\u003e The `arrayFormat: 'comma'` join sink was introduced in qs 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 is the lower boundary; releases before 6.7.0 lack the sink. No released version fixes this vector, and the latest published release (6.16.0) reproduces the throw, so the closed upper bound `\u003c= 6.16.0` covers every affected release. The `encodeValuesOnly` sub-path was added later, but the default comma-join vector already affects 6.7.0, so 6.7.0 governs the lower boundary.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested Fix\n\u003e \n\u003e The comma array serialization must not allow an implicit string coercion of an array element to throw an uncaught exception. Coercion of each comma element should be performed defensively (for example by wrapping the join and per-element encode so that a coercion failure is caught and handled), and this protection must cover both the default `obj.join(',')` path and the `encodeValuesOnly` encoder path, not only the previously patched `null`/`undefined` case. As an interim mitigation without a code change, applications can avoid `arrayFormat: 'comma'` when serializing data derived from untrusted input, sanitize parsed objects (avoid `allowPrototypes`/`plainObjects` on untrusted input and strip unexpected own properties), or wrap `qs.stringify` calls in a synchronous error boundary so a coercion failure cannot crash the process.\n\u003e \n\u003e Reported by zx (GitHub: @manus-pi).\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Independent report by @Lucidsec\n\u003e \n\u003e The following was reported separately, after this advisory, and is merged here as a duplicate.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e In project [qs](https://github.com/ljharb/qs), in versions `\u003e= 6.7.0, \u003c= 6.16.0` (every version that supports `arrayFormat: 'comma'`), `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` can throw an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot convert object to primitive value` when an array element has a non-callable own `toString`. It is fixed in 6.16.1. Related: CWE-248 and CWE-704.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Technical Details\n\u003e \n\u003e `qs.stringify` with `arrayFormat: 'comma'` joins an array at `lib/stringify.js:156` via `obj.join(',')`. An element whose own `toString` is not callable makes `Array.prototype.join` throw. There is no type check and no catch on that path.\n\u003e \n\u003e A second path exists with `encodeValuesOnly`: at `lib/stringify.js:151-154`, `maybeMap` only skips `v == null`, then `lib/utils.js:292` does `String(str)` and throws the same `TypeError` (this second site exists since v6.11.1; before that, the same input throws at `join`).\n\u003e \n\u003e Who can trigger: someone who can shape an array element passed to `stringify`. The host must use non-default `arrayFormat: 'comma'`. Default `arrayFormat` `indices` does not fold arrays and is not affected. A parse-then-stringify path also needs `plainObjects: true` (null-prototype elements from `lib/parse.js:200`) or `allowPrototypes: true` (which Express 4's default query parser, Express 5's `extended` query parser, and body-parser's `extended` urlencoded parser all set). Default `qs.parse('a[0][toString]=x')` returns `{}` because the prototype guard drops `toString`. Direct JSON handed to `stringify` can supply `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Cause: the comma fold at `lib/stringify.js:149-156` collapses the array into a single value before the main loop at `lib/stringify.js:172-212`, so `skipNulls` and `strictNullHandling` only ever see the joined value, never individual elements. `isNonNullishPrimitive` / `isBuffer` at `lib/stringify.js:134` inspect the whole value, not each element. A normal object uses `Object.prototype.toString` and becomes `[object Object]`. An own non-callable `toString` is skipped, `valueOf` returns the object, and `join` throws. (The skipped TODO test at `test/stringify.js:684-689` uses a plain `{ b: 1 }` element, which does not throw; it covers the separate question of what comma format should output for object elements, not this crash.) Earlier comma crash fixes did not cover object elements.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e One small input makes that serialization path throw a synchronous, catchable `TypeError`. Behind a framework error boundary (eg, a synchronous Express handler), the request fails with a 500 and the process survives. In a host with no error boundary (eg, a bare `http.createServer` handler), the uncaught exception exits the process (the client sees `ECONNRESET`). Availability only.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of Concept\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003e \u003csummary\u003eShow the proof of concept\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e Checked on the latest commit that was checked: `07b1d4d82c8f9301c105ea4b94fa2302cfd6e8b4`. Still present. Demonstrated.\n\u003e \n\u003e - Payload `{\"a\":[{\"toString\":\"x\"}]}` throws at `lib/stringify.js:156`.\n\u003e - Baseline `{\"a\":[\"x\",\"y\"]}` returns 200 with body `a=x%2Cy`.\n\u003e - A plain object element stringifies to `a=%5Bobject%20Object%5D` and does not throw.\n\u003e - `encodeValuesOnly: true` throws via `utils.js:292`.\n\u003e - `plainObjects` parse plus comma throws.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003c/details\u003e\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Suggested Fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Before `join`, map elements with the existing primitive check; skip or replace non-primitives so `join` cannot throw. On the `encodeValuesOnly` path, do not send non-primitives into `String()`. Optionally guard `lib/utils.js:292`. Add new regression tests.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | qs | 6.16.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6pq8-jhm2-2vcw | qs | 6.16.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110816\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-111769",
              "name": "CVE-2026-111769",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-111769: MCP TypeScript client SDK: HTTP client transports and OAuth requests followed cross-origin redirects and re-sent custom headers and request bodies"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Summary\nIn affected versions, the SDK's HTTP client transports and OAuth requests followed redirects to any origin.\n\nIf an MCP server or authorization server redirected a request to another origin, that origin received custom request headers (such as an `X-API-Key` set in `requestInit.headers`) and the `mcp-session-id` header. On a `307` or `308` it also received the request body, including OAuth token requests with their `refresh_token` and `client_secret`.\n\nOn current runtimes `fetch` already drops the `Authorization` header across origins.\n\n### Am I affected?\nYes, if your application uses `StreamableHTTPClientTransport`, `SSEClientTransport`, or the SDK's OAuth client functions such as `auth()`. stdio clients are not affected.\n\nAffected versions:\n\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` all versions through 1.31.0\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n\n### Fix\nUpgrade to:\n\n- 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n- 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.3.0 or later\n\nRedirects are now followed only within the same origin, and not at all in browsers. If your MCP server or authorization server redirects to another origin on purpose, configure the final URL instead. Setting `redirectPolicy: 'follow'` on a transport restores the old behaviour and the exposure.\n\nIf a redirect may have reached a host you do not control, rotate the API keys and `client_secret` your application held and revoke its tokens.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade yet, pass a `fetch` that refuses redirects, for example `(url, init) =\u003e fetch(url, { ...init, redirect: 'error' })`.",
                "markdown": "### Summary\nIn affected versions, the SDK's HTTP client transports and OAuth requests followed redirects to any origin.\n\nIf an MCP server or authorization server redirected a request to another origin, that origin received custom request headers (such as an `X-API-Key` set in `requestInit.headers`) and the `mcp-session-id` header. On a `307` or `308` it also received the request body, including OAuth token requests with their `refresh_token` and `client_secret`.\n\nOn current runtimes `fetch` already drops the `Authorization` header across origins.\n\n### Am I affected?\nYes, if your application uses `StreamableHTTPClientTransport`, `SSEClientTransport`, or the SDK's OAuth client functions such as `auth()`. stdio clients are not affected.\n\nAffected versions:\n\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` all versions through 1.31.0\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n\n### Fix\nUpgrade to:\n\n- 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n- 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.3.0 or later\n\nRedirects are now followed only within the same origin, and not at all in browsers. If your MCP server or authorization server redirects to another origin on purpose, configure the final URL instead. Setting `redirectPolicy: 'follow'` on a transport restores the old behaviour and the exposure.\n\nIf a redirect may have reached a host you do not control, rotate the API keys and `client_secret` your application held and revoke its tokens.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade yet, pass a `fetch` that refuses redirects, for example `(url, init) =\u003e fetch(url, { ...init, redirect: 'error' })`."
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111769",
                "GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111769](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111769)**.\n\n## [GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e In affected versions, the SDK's HTTP client transports and OAuth requests followed redirects to any origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e If an MCP server or authorization server redirected a request to another origin, that origin received custom request headers (such as an `X-API-Key` set in `requestInit.headers`) and the `mcp-session-id` header. On a `307` or `308` it also received the request body, including OAuth token requests with their `refresh_token` and `client_secret`.\n\u003e \n\u003e On current runtimes `fetch` already drops the `Authorization` header across origins.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Am I affected?\n\u003e Yes, if your application uses `StreamableHTTPClientTransport`, `SSEClientTransport`, or the SDK's OAuth client functions such as `auth()`. stdio clients are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected versions:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` all versions through 1.31.0\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix\n\u003e Upgrade to:\n\u003e \n\u003e - 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n\u003e - 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.3.0 or later\n\u003e \n\u003e Redirects are now followed only within the same origin, and not at all in browsers. If your MCP server or authorization server redirects to another origin on purpose, configure the final URL instead. Setting `redirectPolicy: 'follow'` on a transport restores the old behaviour and the exposure.\n\u003e \n\u003e If a redirect may have reached a host you do not control, rotate the API keys and `client_secret` your application held and revoke its tokens.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade yet, pass a `fetch` that refuses redirects, for example `(url, init) =\u003e fetch(url, { ...init, redirect: 'error' })`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.29.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.32.0 |\n| GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw | @modelcontextprotocol/client | 2.3.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111769\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111769](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111769)**.\n\n## [GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e In affected versions, the SDK's HTTP client transports and OAuth requests followed redirects to any origin.\n\u003e \n\u003e If an MCP server or authorization server redirected a request to another origin, that origin received custom request headers (such as an `X-API-Key` set in `requestInit.headers`) and the `mcp-session-id` header. On a `307` or `308` it also received the request body, including OAuth token requests with their `refresh_token` and `client_secret`.\n\u003e \n\u003e On current runtimes `fetch` already drops the `Authorization` header across origins.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Am I affected?\n\u003e Yes, if your application uses `StreamableHTTPClientTransport`, `SSEClientTransport`, or the SDK's OAuth client functions such as `auth()`. stdio clients are not affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected versions:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` all versions through 1.31.0\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix\n\u003e Upgrade to:\n\u003e \n\u003e - 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n\u003e - 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/client` 2.3.0 or later\n\u003e \n\u003e Redirects are now followed only within the same origin, and not at all in browsers. If your MCP server or authorization server redirects to another origin on purpose, configure the final URL instead. Setting `redirectPolicy: 'follow'` on a transport restores the old behaviour and the exposure.\n\u003e \n\u003e If a redirect may have reached a host you do not control, rotate the API keys and `client_secret` your application held and revoke its tokens.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade yet, pass a `fetch` that refuses redirects, for example `(url, init) =\u003e fetch(url, { ...init, redirect: 'error' })`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.29.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.32.0 |\n| GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw | @modelcontextprotocol/client | 2.3.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111769\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
