            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-112946",
              "name": "CVE-2026-112946",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-112946: Vite: Files outside `server.fs.allow` could be served when project paths match system paths on non-Windows machine"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Summary\n\nThe content of files outside `server.fs` setting can be returned to the browser if the project imports a file path concatenated with the `root`. For example, if the project references `\u003cpath-to-project-root\u003e/etc/hosts`, the content of `/etc/hosts` was possible to be returned.\n\nThe content of files outside the `server.fs.allow` option can be returned to the browser when a project imports a file whose root-relative path matches an absolute path on the host. For example, referencing `\u003cproject-root\u003e/etc/hosts` could make Vite return the host's `/etc/hosts` file.\n\n### Impact\n\nOnly apps that meet all of the following conditions are affected:\n\n- explicitly expose the Vite dev server to the network (using `--host` or the [`server.host` option](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-host))\n- contain an imported file whose root-relative browser URL is also the absolute path of a readable file on the host\n- The sensitive file is not matched by [`server.fs.deny`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-fs-deny)\n- the dev server is not running on Windows\n\n### Details\n\nWhen Vite analyzes an in-root import, it rewrites the resolved module ID to a browser URL such as `/etc/hostname`. In affected versions, Vite converts that URL with `fsPathFromUrl` and adds the result to `safeModulePaths`. Because `fsPathFromUrl` ensures a leading slash without resolving the URL against the project root, the project URL `/etc/hostname` is recorded as if it were the absolute filesystem path `/etc/hostname`.\n\nFor a later request to `/@fs/etc/hostname`, the raw-filesystem middleware interprets `/etc/hostname` as an absolute path. The access check applies `server.fs.deny`, then accepts paths present in `safeModulePaths` before checking `server.fs.allow`. As a result, the request can return the host file instead of the project file that originally populated `safeModulePaths`.\n\n### PoC\nThe following example requires Linux with a readable `/etc/hostname`:\n```bash\n$ mkdir vite-safe-module-path-poc\n$ cd vite-safe-module-path-poc\n$ npm init -y\n$ npm install vite@8.3.1\n$ mkdir -p etc\n$ echo 'PROJECT-DECOY\\n' \u003e etc/hostname\n$ echo 'import \"./etc/hostname\"\\n' \u003e main.js\n$ npx vite --host 127.0.0.1\n```\n\nIn another terminal:\n```bash\n$ curl -o /dev/null -s -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/main.js\n200\n$ curl -sS -o response -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/@fs/etc/hostname\n200\n$ cmp -s response /etc/hostname \u0026\u0026 echo 'response matches host file'\nresponse matches host file\n```",
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\nThe content of files outside `server.fs` setting can be returned to the browser if the project imports a file path concatenated with the `root`. For example, if the project references `\u003cpath-to-project-root\u003e/etc/hosts`, the content of `/etc/hosts` was possible to be returned.\n\nThe content of files outside the `server.fs.allow` option can be returned to the browser when a project imports a file whose root-relative path matches an absolute path on the host. For example, referencing `\u003cproject-root\u003e/etc/hosts` could make Vite return the host's `/etc/hosts` file.\n\n### Impact\n\nOnly apps that meet all of the following conditions are affected:\n\n- explicitly expose the Vite dev server to the network (using `--host` or the [`server.host` option](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-host))\n- contain an imported file whose root-relative browser URL is also the absolute path of a readable file on the host\n- The sensitive file is not matched by [`server.fs.deny`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-fs-deny)\n- the dev server is not running on Windows\n\n### Details\n\nWhen Vite analyzes an in-root import, it rewrites the resolved module ID to a browser URL such as `/etc/hostname`. In affected versions, Vite converts that URL with `fsPathFromUrl` and adds the result to `safeModulePaths`. Because `fsPathFromUrl` ensures a leading slash without resolving the URL against the project root, the project URL `/etc/hostname` is recorded as if it were the absolute filesystem path `/etc/hostname`.\n\nFor a later request to `/@fs/etc/hostname`, the raw-filesystem middleware interprets `/etc/hostname` as an absolute path. The access check applies `server.fs.deny`, then accepts paths present in `safeModulePaths` before checking `server.fs.allow`. As a result, the request can return the host file instead of the project file that originally populated `safeModulePaths`.\n\n### PoC\nThe following example requires Linux with a readable `/etc/hostname`:\n```bash\n$ mkdir vite-safe-module-path-poc\n$ cd vite-safe-module-path-poc\n$ npm init -y\n$ npm install vite@8.3.1\n$ mkdir -p etc\n$ echo 'PROJECT-DECOY\\n' \u003e etc/hostname\n$ echo 'import \"./etc/hostname\"\\n' \u003e main.js\n$ npx vite --host 127.0.0.1\n```\n\nIn another terminal:\n```bash\n$ curl -o /dev/null -s -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/main.js\n200\n$ curl -sS -o response -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/@fs/etc/hostname\n200\n$ cmp -s response /etc/hostname \u0026\u0026 echo 'response matches host file'\nresponse matches host file\n```"
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-112946",
                "GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112946](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-112946)**.\n\n## [GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The content of files outside `server.fs` setting can be returned to the browser if the project imports a file path concatenated with the `root`. For example, if the project references `\u003cpath-to-project-root\u003e/etc/hosts`, the content of `/etc/hosts` was possible to be returned.\n\u003e \n\u003e The content of files outside the `server.fs.allow` option can be returned to the browser when a project imports a file whose root-relative path matches an absolute path on the host. For example, referencing `\u003cproject-root\u003e/etc/hosts` could make Vite return the host's `/etc/hosts` file.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only apps that meet all of the following conditions are affected:\n\u003e \n\u003e - explicitly expose the Vite dev server to the network (using `--host` or the [`server.host` option](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-host))\n\u003e - contain an imported file whose root-relative browser URL is also the absolute path of a readable file on the host\n\u003e - The sensitive file is not matched by [`server.fs.deny`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-fs-deny)\n\u003e - the dev server is not running on Windows\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e When Vite analyzes an in-root import, it rewrites the resolved module ID to a browser URL such as `/etc/hostname`. In affected versions, Vite converts that URL with `fsPathFromUrl` and adds the result to `safeModulePaths`. Because `fsPathFromUrl` ensures a leading slash without resolving the URL against the project root, the project URL `/etc/hostname` is recorded as if it were the absolute filesystem path `/etc/hostname`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For a later request to `/@fs/etc/hostname`, the raw-filesystem middleware interprets `/etc/hostname` as an absolute path. The access check applies `server.fs.deny`, then accepts paths present in `safeModulePaths` before checking `server.fs.allow`. As a result, the request can return the host file instead of the project file that originally populated `safeModulePaths`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e The following example requires Linux with a readable `/etc/hostname`:\n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e $ mkdir vite-safe-module-path-poc\n\u003e $ cd vite-safe-module-path-poc\n\u003e $ npm init -y\n\u003e $ npm install vite@8.3.1\n\u003e $ mkdir -p etc\n\u003e $ echo 'PROJECT-DECOY\\n' \u003e etc/hostname\n\u003e $ echo 'import \"./etc/hostname\"\\n' \u003e main.js\n\u003e $ npx vite --host 127.0.0.1\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In another terminal:\n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e $ curl -o /dev/null -s -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/main.js\n\u003e 200\n\u003e $ curl -sS -o response -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/@fs/etc/hostname\n\u003e 200\n\u003e $ cmp -s response /etc/hostname \u0026\u0026 echo 'response matches host file'\n\u003e response matches host file\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | vite | 6.4.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22 | vite | 6.4.4, 7.3.7, 8.1.6, 8.2.4, 8.3.3 |\n| GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22 | vite-plus | 1.1.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112946\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-112946](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-112946)**.\n\n## [GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e The content of files outside `server.fs` setting can be returned to the browser if the project imports a file path concatenated with the `root`. For example, if the project references `\u003cpath-to-project-root\u003e/etc/hosts`, the content of `/etc/hosts` was possible to be returned.\n\u003e \n\u003e The content of files outside the `server.fs.allow` option can be returned to the browser when a project imports a file whose root-relative path matches an absolute path on the host. For example, referencing `\u003cproject-root\u003e/etc/hosts` could make Vite return the host's `/etc/hosts` file.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e Only apps that meet all of the following conditions are affected:\n\u003e \n\u003e - explicitly expose the Vite dev server to the network (using `--host` or the [`server.host` option](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-host))\n\u003e - contain an imported file whose root-relative browser URL is also the absolute path of a readable file on the host\n\u003e - The sensitive file is not matched by [`server.fs.deny`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-fs-deny)\n\u003e - the dev server is not running on Windows\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e When Vite analyzes an in-root import, it rewrites the resolved module ID to a browser URL such as `/etc/hostname`. In affected versions, Vite converts that URL with `fsPathFromUrl` and adds the result to `safeModulePaths`. Because `fsPathFromUrl` ensures a leading slash without resolving the URL against the project root, the project URL `/etc/hostname` is recorded as if it were the absolute filesystem path `/etc/hostname`.\n\u003e \n\u003e For a later request to `/@fs/etc/hostname`, the raw-filesystem middleware interprets `/etc/hostname` as an absolute path. The access check applies `server.fs.deny`, then accepts paths present in `safeModulePaths` before checking `server.fs.allow`. As a result, the request can return the host file instead of the project file that originally populated `safeModulePaths`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### PoC\n\u003e The following example requires Linux with a readable `/etc/hostname`:\n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e $ mkdir vite-safe-module-path-poc\n\u003e $ cd vite-safe-module-path-poc\n\u003e $ npm init -y\n\u003e $ npm install vite@8.3.1\n\u003e $ mkdir -p etc\n\u003e $ echo 'PROJECT-DECOY\\n' \u003e etc/hostname\n\u003e $ echo 'import \"./etc/hostname\"\\n' \u003e main.js\n\u003e $ npx vite --host 127.0.0.1\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e In another terminal:\n\u003e ```bash\n\u003e $ curl -o /dev/null -s -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/main.js\n\u003e 200\n\u003e $ curl -sS -o response -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:5173/@fs/etc/hostname\n\u003e 200\n\u003e $ cmp -s response /etc/hostname \u0026\u0026 echo 'response matches host file'\n\u003e response matches host file\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | vite | 6.4.3 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22 | vite | 6.4.4, 7.3.7, 8.1.6, 8.2.4, 8.3.3 |\n| GHSA-rq7h-c2jc-7f22 | vite-plus | 1.1.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-112946\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-111771",
              "name": "CVE-2026-111771",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-111771: MCP TypeScript SDK: Server bearer authentication accepted tokens issued for another service"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Summary\nIn affected versions, the SDK's server-side bearer authentication had no way to check that a token was issued for your server (its audience). Where one authorization server issues tokens for several services, a valid token for another service was also accepted by your MCP server.\n\n### Am I affected?\nYes, if your HTTP server uses `requireBearerAuth()` or `verifyBearerToken()`, its authorization server also issues tokens for other services, and your `verifyAccessToken()` does not check the token's audience (`aud`) itself.\n\nAffected versions:\n\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.6.0 through 1.31.0\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.0 through 2.0.1\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.0.0 through 2.3.0\n\n### Fix\nUpgrade to:\n\n- 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n- 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.3.0 or later, together with `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.2 or later if you use it\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.3.1 or later\n\n**Upgrading alone changes nothing.** The patched versions add an `expectedResource` setting that is off by default. To turn the check on, make both of these changes together:\n\n1. Pass the audience your authorization server puts in tokens for your server: `requireBearerAuth({ verifier, expectedResource: new URL('https://mcp.example.com/mcp') })`.\n2. Return the token's audience from `verifyAccessToken()` as `AuthInfo.resource`, also a `URL`.\n\nA token for another service then gets `401`. With only the first change, every request gets `401`.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade yet, reject any token whose `aud` does not include your server inside `verifyAccessToken()`. That closes the issue on every version.",
                "markdown": "### Summary\nIn affected versions, the SDK's server-side bearer authentication had no way to check that a token was issued for your server (its audience). Where one authorization server issues tokens for several services, a valid token for another service was also accepted by your MCP server.\n\n### Am I affected?\nYes, if your HTTP server uses `requireBearerAuth()` or `verifyBearerToken()`, its authorization server also issues tokens for other services, and your `verifyAccessToken()` does not check the token's audience (`aud`) itself.\n\nAffected versions:\n\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.6.0 through 1.31.0\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.0 through 2.0.1\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.0.0 through 2.3.0\n\n### Fix\nUpgrade to:\n\n- 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n- 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.3.0 or later, together with `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.2 or later if you use it\n- `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.3.1 or later\n\n**Upgrading alone changes nothing.** The patched versions add an `expectedResource` setting that is off by default. To turn the check on, make both of these changes together:\n\n1. Pass the audience your authorization server puts in tokens for your server: `requireBearerAuth({ verifier, expectedResource: new URL('https://mcp.example.com/mcp') })`.\n2. Return the token's audience from `verifyAccessToken()` as `AuthInfo.resource`, also a `URL`.\n\nA token for another service then gets `401`. With only the first change, every request gets `401`.\n\nIf you cannot upgrade yet, reject any token whose `aud` does not include your server inside `verifyAccessToken()`. That closes the issue on every version."
              },
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-111771",
                "GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq"
              ],
              "help": {
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111771](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111771)**.\n\n## [GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e In affected versions, the SDK's server-side bearer authentication had no way to check that a token was issued for your server (its audience). Where one authorization server issues tokens for several services, a valid token for another service was also accepted by your MCP server.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Am I affected?\n\u003e Yes, if your HTTP server uses `requireBearerAuth()` or `verifyBearerToken()`, its authorization server also issues tokens for other services, and your `verifyAccessToken()` does not check the token's audience (`aud`) itself.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected versions:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.6.0 through 1.31.0\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.0 through 2.0.1\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.0.0 through 2.3.0\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix\n\u003e Upgrade to:\n\u003e \n\u003e - 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n\u003e - 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.3.0 or later, together with `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.2 or later if you use it\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.3.1 or later\n\u003e \n\u003e **Upgrading alone changes nothing.** The patched versions add an `expectedResource` setting that is off by default. To turn the check on, make both of these changes together:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Pass the audience your authorization server puts in tokens for your server: `requireBearerAuth({ verifier, expectedResource: new URL('https://mcp.example.com/mcp') })`.\n\u003e 2. Return the token's audience from `verifyAccessToken()` as `AuthInfo.resource`, also a `URL`.\n\u003e \n\u003e A token for another service then gets `401`. With only the first change, every request gets `401`.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade yet, reject any token whose `aud` does not include your server inside `verifyAccessToken()`. That closes the issue on every version.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.29.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.32.0 |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/server | 2.3.0 |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/express | 2.0.2 |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy | 2.3.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111771\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111771](https://osv.dev/list?q=CVE-2026-111771)**.\n\n## [GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e In affected versions, the SDK's server-side bearer authentication had no way to check that a token was issued for your server (its audience). Where one authorization server issues tokens for several services, a valid token for another service was also accepted by your MCP server.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Am I affected?\n\u003e Yes, if your HTTP server uses `requireBearerAuth()` or `verifyBearerToken()`, its authorization server also issues tokens for other services, and your `verifyAccessToken()` does not check the token's audience (`aud`) itself.\n\u003e \n\u003e Affected versions:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.6.0 through 1.31.0\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.0.0 through 2.2.0\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.0 through 2.0.1\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.0.0 through 2.3.0\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Fix\n\u003e Upgrade to:\n\u003e \n\u003e - 1.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.32.0 or later\n\u003e - 2.x: `@modelcontextprotocol/server` 2.3.0 or later, together with `@modelcontextprotocol/express` 2.0.2 or later if you use it\n\u003e - `@modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy` 2.3.1 or later\n\u003e \n\u003e **Upgrading alone changes nothing.** The patched versions add an `expectedResource` setting that is off by default. To turn the check on, make both of these changes together:\n\u003e \n\u003e 1. Pass the audience your authorization server puts in tokens for your server: `requireBearerAuth({ verifier, expectedResource: new URL('https://mcp.example.com/mcp') })`.\n\u003e 2. Return the token's audience from `verifyAccessToken()` as `AuthInfo.resource`, also a `URL`.\n\u003e \n\u003e A token for another service then gets `401`. With only the first change, every request gets `401`.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you cannot upgrade yet, reject any token whose `aud` does not include your server inside `verifyAccessToken()`. That closes the issue on every version.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/package-lock.json | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.29.0 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/sdk | 1.32.0 |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/server | 2.3.0 |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/express | 2.0.2 |\n| GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq | @modelcontextprotocol/server-legacy | 2.3.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/builds/iw/ai/agent-kit/ironwallet-agent-kit/packages/mcp-server/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111771\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              }
